nciple when it comes to strength  training is that you'll be using big  compound lifts the reason for this is of  a compound lift such as the deadlift  such as the bench press uses lots of  muscles together in order to exert more  force if you can exert more force that  means you can lift heavier weights and  if you want to get stronger then lifting  more is the objective at the same time  this teaches your body to use those  muscles all together as a single  cohesive unit and that in turn means  you're more functional you can transfer  that strength more to the real-world  training programs like strong lifts 5  times 5 are built around these  principles and are a great starting  point if your main goal is to build  strength in those key lifts however if  we really want to be super strong like  The Incredible Hulk then of course we  want to take things a little bit further  first of all we want to define strength  there is of course a difference between  absolute strength and relative strength  absolute strength is the total strength  that you have the amount of weight that  you can shift relative strength on the  other hand is your strength to weight  ratio we can also divide strength into  max strength also called limit strength  which sounds awesome starting strength  and strength endurance and these three  different things describe different  facets of your strengths so max strength  that's the peak amount of force you can  generate at any given point I have a  starting strength in some ways is more  important for athletes this is the speed  at which you can generate a huge amount  of force and there's a kind of  acceleration curve you have strength  endurance which of course is your  ability to exert strength over a  significant amount of time now imagine  that you're an Incredible Hulk and  you're holding up the roof what  everybody else is escaping and how long  you can hold that for that's your  strength endurance it's no use being  really strong once so how do we train  these things well there's a few  different methods which of course I'm  about to share now if you want to  improve your starting strength then you  can use something called the dynamic  efforts method and this is going to  improve our rate of force development  which is that initial burst of the  starting blocks do this we're going to  be using slightly lighter weights and  we're going to be focusing on the speed  at which we go through the motion the  ideal amount of weight is 50 to 70  percent of your one rep maximum so for  instance if you normally benchpress a  hundred kilograms you're going to take  50 kilograms and they're going to  perform the movement but you're gonna  focus on going as fast as possible and  exploding through that movement I really  like what Ross Edgeley says which is to  imagine that you're trying to launch the  bar through the ceiling but of course  don't let go you'll be banned from your  gym when you do this you're using  something called compensatory  acceleration in other words you're still  producing the same amount of force he  would be for the heavy weight but now  some of that is in the form of  acceleration rather than just lifting  that amount of weight and what this  should do is to improve your neural  Drive for that you can recruit two  muscle fiber more quickly improve your  rate coding so they're firing faster  thus giving you the speed initially in  the movement if you've improved your  squat a huge amount but you haven't  improved your vertical leap then using  this kind of training could be what's  missing that and you might also be too  heavy which is why you can also use  plyometrics the west side the barbell  system which is where the dynamic  efforts method comes from recommends  doing two days of max effort heavy  weights and two days of dynamic effort I  just recommend that if you are doing  some kind of training program focused  purely on strength on the big lifts that  you maybe do incorporate at least one  dynamic effort day in there or just use  the mind muscle connection to really  focus on exploding through those  movements sometimes another way we can  increase our muscle fiber recruitment is  through ballistic isometrics i've talked  about this before basically you're doing  in over  i symmetric i means you're pushing or  pulling against something you can't  shift for instance loads too much weight  onto the bench press so you can't lift  it then use the rack to have it low at a  point where your arms are bent to have  it in your grip now you're going to  attempt to press it but obviously you're  not going to be able to do it ideally  you want to contract for six seconds  each repetition and you need to go  through three different joint angles so  you're gonna have it lower around the  middle and then arms nearly extended and  the reason for this is that isometrics  only effect thirty degrees of the joint  angle range of motion ballistic aspect  of this means that you're going to be  trying to burst into that movement  you're gonna push as hard as you can all  of a sudden and you can do this in a  kind of pulse method safe burst into the  movement hold it another burst another  burst and of course what you're doing  here is telling your body to recruit  those big muscle fibers in order to  generate that force and acceleration of  course nothing's going to happen but  it's the intent that creates that  important connection this is something  that Bruce Lee used and the old-time  strongmen used and it's fantastic for  building real power was the isometrics  on the other hand and slow eccentrics  are useful if you want to build that  strength endurance so strength endurance  is not quite the same as muscle  endurance it's not quite the same as  doing high repetitions with a lower  weight not the same as bodybuilding  style pump training although of course  there's some crossover what we're gonna  be doing it's gonna be using heavy  weights till seventy to eighty percent  we're gonna be doing more volume per  session and we're going to be reducing  the recovery time in between sets this  will allow you to use more muscle for  longer and more frequently but what I  also recommend doing is slowing down the  actual movement and one way you can do  this is with very slow eccentrics  because the eccentric portion of the  movement the lengthening of the muscle  normally the part we are putting the  weight down that's where you're  strongest and if you think about the  example of Hulk holding up the roof  what's he doing is preventing his  muscles from elongating he's fighting  against the eccentric portion of the  movement so by very slowly lowering  weights they're extremely heavy you can  build up your strength and endurance and  exert force for longer and quasi  isometrics take this concept to the  extreme by challenging you to perform a  range of motion taking about a minute to  do so so of course it gonna really drop  down the weight now you might even just  use your body weight you're gonna do  something like a pull-up or a squat and  you're gonna take an entire minute or  even longer to perform the whole  movement and what this is going to do is  it's going to improve your mind muscle  connection  feel yourself moving at the same time  it's gonna make sure that there's really  no points of weakness in that motion if  you go through a squat normally using a  heavy weight and fully explosive  movement and you can actually use  momentum through quite a lot of the  range of motion that means that if  you've got a sticking point a weak area  you can just power on past it and never  have to deal with it if you slow things  right down you can really feel your  technique all the way down and you can  feel where you're weakest and this  allows you to work on that thus making  you much stronger overall finally one  other technique I recommend for  improving your max strength in  particular is heavy partials so  extremely heavy 